{"name":"Pisco Sour","hex":"#beeb71","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"pisco sour","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A Peruvian cocktail containing pisco, lemon or lime juice, egg whites, simple syrup, and bitters."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/pisco_sour"}],"entries":[{"title":"Pisco sour","blocks":[{"text":"A pisco sour is an alcoholic cocktail of Peruvian origin, which is made from pisco, a Peruvian brandy, with sour citrus juice and sweetener components. The Peruvian pisco sour includes Peruvian pisco with freshly squeezed lime juice, simple syrup, ice, egg white, and Angostura bitters. The Chilean version is similar, but has Chilean pisco and Pica lime, and excludes the bitters and egg white. Other variants of the cocktail include with fruits like pineapple or plants such as coca leaves."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/27/Pisco_sour_20100613b.JPG/330px-Pisco_sour_20100613b.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pisco_sour"},{"title":"Pisco","blocks":[{"text":"Pisco is a colorless or yellowish-to-amber-colored spirit produced in winemaking regions of Peru and Chile. Made by distilling fermented grape juice into a high-proof spirit, it was developed by 16th-century Spanish settlers as an alternative to orujo, a pomace brandy that was being imported from Spain. It had the advantages of being produced from abundant domestically grown fruit and reducing the volume of alcoholic beverages transported to remote locations."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c9/Range_of_Pisco.jpg/330px-Range_of_Pisco.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pisco"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/27/Pisco_sour_20100613b.JPG/500px-Pisco_sour_20100613b.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/27/Pisco_sour_20100613b.JPG/1280px-Pisco_sour_20100613b.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/27/Pisco_sour_20100613b.JPG/500px-Pisco_sour_20100613b.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APisco_sour_20100613b.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8e/Plaza_Acho.jpg/500px-Plaza_Acho.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8e/Plaza_Acho.jpg/500px-Plaza_Acho.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":" The Plaza de toros de Acho (Lima), the oldest bullring in the Americas, where the predecessor to the pisco sour was sold","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APlaza_Acho.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0a/VVM_Younger.jpg/330px-VVM_Younger.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0a/VVM_Younger.jpg/330px-VVM_Younger.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Victor Morris, inventor of the pisco sour","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AVVM_Younger.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/75/Morris-Bar-Pisco-Sour-South-Pacific-Mail-1924.JPG/120px-Morris-Bar-Pisco-Sour-South-Pacific-Mail-1924.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/75/Morris-Bar-Pisco-Sour-South-Pacific-Mail-1924.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/